  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nPersistent stratus around 4k ft was lingering across portions of \nsouth central KS this morning while slowly mixing out while mostly \nclear conditions prevailed elsewhere. Southerly flow has returned to \ncentral and western KS where breezy southwest winds were common. We \ncontinue to see a shortwave trough racing eastward along the \nCanadian border. A trailing cold front extended SSW from this trough \nacross the Northern Plains. This frontal boundary is progged to \ncontinue moving southward arriving across the region tonight. This \nfront will usher in some drier air while keeping seasonable \ntemperatures and tranquil weather conditions in place as we move \ninto Sunday-Monday.\n\nRising heights/increasing thickness will result in moderating \ntemperatures as we move into Tue and beyond. We could see another \nsubtle frontal boundary move into the area late Tue into early Wed, \nalthough there remains some uncertainty in how far south this cold \nfront settles before becoming stationary,so seasonably mild (above \naverage) temperatures were maintained as we move through the middle \nof the week. By late in the week, the large-scale pattern is progged \nto transition away from NW mid/upper flow as a mid/upper trough \nbuilds into the Pacific NW and another mid/upper trough develops \nacross the Southern Plains. This feature could bring some unsettled \nweather as we move into the weekend but higher probabilities may \nremain south of the area with some uncertainty in how quickly this \nfeature lifts north and eastward. Dry and mild conditions are \nexpected to prevail through the period at this time.",
